<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Lomax</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1867">1867</a>–<a href="/years/1948">1948</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as John Avery Lomax, John A. Lomax</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Lomax was an American teacher, musicologist and folklorist who worked to preserve American folk music. Raised in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, he studied at the University of Texas and Harvard, where George Lyman Kittredge urged him to collect ballads in the field; his Cowboy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Songs</a> and Other Frontier Ballads appeared in 1910 with an introduction by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-roosevelt">Theodore Roosevelt</a>. From 1933 he made recording trips for the Library of Congress with his son Alan, recording Huddie Ledbetter, Lead Belly, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> State Penitentiary, and from 1934 until his death he was honorary curator of the Archive of American Folk Song. He also directed the gathering of ex-slave narratives for the Federal Writers' Project.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-lomax.jpg" alt="John Lomax" width="330" height="382" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ruby Terrill Lomax · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John-avery-lomax1_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The folklorist <b>John Lomax</b> printed it in 1910 in Cowboy Songs and Other Frontier Ballads, and reported that he had learned it from a Black saloon keeper in Texas who remembered it from the Chisholm Trail.</blockquote><a href="/songs/home-on-the-range">Home on the Range</a></li><li><blockquote>In May 1936 <b>John Lomax</b> found Ethel Best singing &quot;Come by Here&quot; with a group in Raiford, Florida.</blockquote><a href="/songs/kumbaya">Kumbaya</a></li><li><blockquote>In the United States <b>John Lomax</b> recorded a related version, &quot;The Cambric Shirt&quot;, from Georgia Ann Griffin in Florida in 1939, and other field recordings followed in Arkansas (1958), New York (1966) and Bristol (1976).</blockquote><a href="/songs/scarborough-fair">Scarborough Fair</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Goodman, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in Greenville, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.97,-89.91&amp;year=1919" aria-label="Goodman on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2247 966 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of John Lomax"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2697 1236h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2697 1236h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=32.97,-89.91&amp;year=1919">What was here in 1919? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
